    "topic_title": "Calculating Higher-Order Derivatives",
    "stem": "Let f(x) = x⁴ − 3x³ + 2x. What is f″(2)?",
    "choices": [
     {
      "choice_key": "A",
      "choice_text": "12",
      "is_correct": true,
      "rationale": "Differentiate twice: f′(x) = 4x³ − 9x² + 2 and f″(x) = 12x² − 18x. Then f″(2) = 48 − 36 = 12."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "B",
      "choice_text": "−2",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "Stopping after one derivative gives f′(2) = −2, not f″(2). The double prime requires differentiating the original function twice. Fix: Write f′(x), then differentiate it before substituting x = 2."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "C",
      "choice_text": "30",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "Differentiating one time too many gives f‴(x) = 24x − 18 and f‴(2) = 30. The question asks for the second derivative, not the third. Fix: Count the primes and stop after that many differentiations."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "D",
      "choice_text": "0",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "Substituting too early gives the constant f(2) = −4, whose derivatives are zero. Differentiating this constant does not compute the derivatives of f at x = 2. Fix: Differentiate the function twice before evaluating at x = 2."
     }
    ]
